    ["video_files",
    ".264",
    "Ripped Video Data File",
    "3.5",
    "Temporary video file generated by video ripping and conversion programs, such as StaxRip and RipBot264; contains extracted video data that can be repurposed and encoded into other video formats; typically stored with MPEG-4/H.264 encoding. 264 files are used to store raw, intermediary video data before conversion. Windows StaxRip. RipBot264. Mac Roxio Toast 20. A 264 file contains video data captured by an IP or closed-circuit television (CCTV) camera and recorded on a DVR device. It stores video data that can only be played back with the software delivered with the camera. You most likely will encounter 264 files if you own a camera for security purposes and are trying to view or edit the video. You may also acquire a 264 video file from a client if you are a legal professional working on a case where video evidence is provided. Since 264 files are often meant to store sensitive video, they are typically encoded in a proprietary format only supported by software developed by the camera manufacturer. In this case, there may be software packaged with the camera that can play video saved in 264 files or there may be software available to download from the website of the camera developer. NOTE: You may also be able to view a 264 video file with VideoLAN VLC media player if it is encoded with the H.264 compression codec. If you are able to open it with VLC media player you can convert it to a more widely supported format, such as.MP4 or.MOV. Windows Software packaged with the camera VideoLAN VLC media player. Mac Software packaged with the camera VideoLAN VLC media player. Linux Software packaged with the camera VideoLAN VLC media playerFree.",
    "Windows StaxRip. RipBot264. Mac Roxio Toast 20. Windows Software packaged with the camera VideoLAN VLC media player. Mac Software packaged with the camera VideoLAN VLC media player. Linux Software packaged with the camera VideoLAN VLC media player.",
    "?"],
    ["video_files",
    ".BNP",
    "Sony Camcorder Video Data File",
    "3.5",
    "Data file created by some Sony digital camcorders; stored within the AVF_INFO directory on the camcorder and contains metadata about the recorded videos; used for displaying information on the camcorder such as the dates and number of videos recorded. If you delete the files in the AVF_INFO directory, the recorded camera files will not play back correctly. However, you can still transfer the video data to a computer. Windows Sony Picture Motion Browser —  (Discontinued).",
    "Windows Sony Picture Motion Browser —  (Discontinued).",
    "?"],
    ["video_files",
    ".MTS",
    "AVCHD Video",
    "3.5",
    "An MTS file is a video saved in the Advanced Video Coding High Definition (AVCHD) format. AVCHD is the standard video format used by many Sony and Panasonic HD camcorders. MTS files also store high-definition video on Blu-ray discs. MTS file open in VideoLAN VLC media player 3 MTS is short for MPEG Transport Stream. The file format uses MPEG-4 / H.264 video compression and supports 720p and 1080i HD video resolutions. The audio in an MTS file may be compressed using the Dolby AC-3 codec or saved as uncompressed linear PCM audio. Supported audio tracks include stereo (2-channel) and 5.1 (5-channel + subwoofer) surround sound. MTS videos are saved in the Advanced Video Coding High Definition format. The AVCHD format was established by Sony and Panasonic in 2006 and is used by many of the companies HD camcorders. Most Sony and Panasonic HD camcorders come packaged with software that can open and edit MTS files. NOTE: AVCHD files saved on a camcorder typically use the.mts file extension. AVCHD files edited on a computer or imported from a Blu-ray disc typically use the.M2TS extension. You can open and play MTS videos with a variety of media players, including: VideoLAN VLC media player (cross-platform) Microsoft Windows Media Player (Windows) Eltima Elmedia Player (Mac) You can edit MTS videos using the software that comes packaged with Sony and Panasonic HD camcorders or any of the following programs: Blender (cross-platform) MAGIX Vegas (Windows) CyberLink PowerDirector (Windows, Mac, Android). Mac Roxio Toast 20. Roxio Popcorn. Apple Final Cut Pro. VideoLAN VLC media player. CyberLink PowerDirector 365. Blender. Eltima Elmedia Player. Avidemux. Adobe Premiere Pro 2023. Windows File Viewer Plus — Get it from Microsoft. Microsoft Windows Media Player Included with OS. GRETECH GOM Player. Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. CyberLink PowerDirector 365. MAGIX VEGAS Pro 18. Corel VideoStudio 2022. MAGIX Movie Edit Pro. Grass Valley EDIUS. VideoLAN VLC media player. Blender. Avidemux. Adobe Premiere Pro 2023. Linux OpenShot. VideoLAN VLC media player. Blender. MPlayer. Avidemux. Web Google Drive. Android CyberLink PowerDirector 365. File created by MEGA (Molecular Evolutionary Genetics Analysis), a program used for testing evolutionary hypotheses; saves a phylogenetic tree, which infers ancestral relationships between species based on their genetic makeup. Phylogenetic trees can require extensive amounts of computation based on genetic information, so MTS files allow users to save the computed output without having to recompute the tree again. Mac MEGA. Windows MEGAFree. An MTS file is a sample file used by the MadTracker 2 audio tracking program. It contains an audio sample of an instrument, voice, or another sound. MTS files are assigned to audio tracks and may be played back at different pitches that correspond to notes in the score. MTS file open in MadTracker 2 MadTracker 2 is the second version of the MadTracker audio composition tool. It comes with various features, including built-in effects, VST plugins, samples, and demo songs. How to create a MadTracker 2 Sample MTS file You can create MTS files in MadTracker 2 to save samples that you can then re-use in the software. To create an MTS file, select Sample → Save.., then choose MadTracker Sample from the Save as type dropdown menu. You can open MTS Sample files with MadTracker 2 by selecting Sample → Load... Windows MadTracker 2Free.",
    "Mac Roxio Toast 20. Roxio Popcorn. Apple Final Cut Pro. VideoLAN VLC media player. CyberLink PowerDirector 365. Blender. Eltima Elmedia Player. Avidemux. Adobe Premiere Pro 2023. Windows File Viewer Plus — Get it from Microsoft. Microsoft Windows Media Player Included with OS. GRETECH GOM Player. Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. CyberLink PowerDirector 365. MAGIX VEGAS Pro 18. Corel VideoStudio 2022. MAGIX Movie Edit Pro. Grass Valley EDIUS. VideoLAN VLC media player. Blender. Avidemux. Adobe Premiere Pro 2023. Linux OpenShot. VideoLAN VLC media player. Blender. MPlayer. Avidemux. Web Google Drive. Android CyberLink PowerDirector 365. Mac MEGA. Windows MEGA. Windows MadTracker 2.",
    "Various media players and video editors, including VideoLAN VLC media player and Eltima Elmedia Player, can convert MTS files to other formats. For example, VLC media player can convert MTS files to the following formats:.MP4 - MPEG-4 Video.WEBM - WebM Video.TS - Video Transport Stream.ASF - Advanced Systems Format.WAV - WAVE Audio.OGG - Ogg Vorbis Audio.MP3 - MP3 Audio.FLAC -. Lossless Audio Codec. MadTracker 2 can convert MTS Sample files to the following formats:.WAV - WAVE Audio.AIF - Audio Interchange File Format.IFF - Interchange File Format.OGG - Ogg Vorbis Audio To convert an MTS Sample file with MadTracker 2, select Sample → Save.. and choose the target format from the Save as type dropdown menu."],
    ["video_files",
    ".JDR",
    "IROAD Dash Cam Video File",
    "3.5",
    "A JDR file contains video recorded by an IROAD dash camera. It is saved in the proprietary JDR format and can only be played by PC viewer, which is included with the purchase of IROAD dash cameras, and the IROAD mobile app for Android and iOS. JDR files were introduced with the release of IROAD V9 cameras. JDR file open in IROAD WiFi PC Viewer 3.7 JDR files are automatically created by IROAD dash cameras when beginning a recording and then saved to the cameras SD card. You can insert the SD card into a computer and transfer the files to your computer. The files can be played using the PC Viewer or the IROAD mobile app. NOTE: Since JDR files are not supported by other players, you can also convert JDR files to the more widely supported.AVI format using the IROAD mobile app. Windows IROAD WiFi PC viewer. Mac IROAD WiFi PC viewer. iOS IROAD. Android IROADFree.",
    "Windows IROAD WiFi PC viewer. Mac IROAD WiFi PC viewer. iOS IROAD. Android IROAD.",
    "?"],
    ["video_files",
    ".H264",
    "H.264 Encoded Video File",
    "3.5",
    "An H264 file is a video file encoded with H.264 compression, which is a popular format for high-definition video. It is often used as the video format for AVCHD camcorders, HDTV, Blu-ray, and HD DVD. H264 generally refers to a video file that is an.MP4 file. H264 file open in VideoLAN VLC media player H.264 is a video compression codec that requires a video container to host the encoded video. Therefore, a file with the.h264 extension may be a misnamed.264,.MP4,.AVI, or.MKV file. Nevertheless, the.h264 extension is still sometimes used to describe the contents of H.264 encoded video files. You can play the video an H264 file contains in VideoLAN VLC media player (multiplatform) or File Viewer Plus (Windows). Note that, by default, VLC may not recognize H264 files as valid video files. Therefore, you may have to open your H264 file by selecting File → Open File.. from VLCs menu bar, instead of just double-clicking the file. Windows File Viewer Plus. VideoLAN VLC media player. Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. Corel VideoStudio 2022. CyberLink PowerDVD 21. Mac Roxio Toast 20. VideoLAN VLC media player. Linux VideoLAN VLC media playerFree.",
    "Windows File Viewer Plus. VideoLAN VLC media player. Roxio Creator NXT Pro 9. Corel VideoStudio 2022. CyberLink PowerDVD 21. Mac Roxio Toast 20. VideoLAN VLC media player. Linux VideoLAN VLC media player.",
    "You can use VideoLAN VLC media player (multiplatform) or File Viewer Plus (Windows) to convert H264 videos to other formats. For example, VLC can convert H264 files to:.MP4 - MPEG-4 Video.WEBM - WebM Video.TS - Video Transport Stream.ASF - Advanced Systems Format."],
